<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 2</h2>
<p style="text-align: center;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 9th Grade (CEFR: A2.4)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Wellness and Mindfulness</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> The Benefits of Mindfulness</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Outline the main information from simple diagrams (e.g., graphs, bar charts). Reading: Recognize the language on a simple form asking for personal information.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can outline key points from bar charts on fitness benefits and connect them to physical education and extracurricular activities (e.g., "List two main benefits of regular exercise for students who participate in physical education or extracurricular activities like baseball, soccer, volleyball, and folkloric dance.")
            Can recognize the language used on wellness questionnaires (e.g., "On the health check-in form, I need to fill out my name, age, and how many hours I sleep each night.")
            Can outline information from graphs on stress-reduction techniques (e.g., "The graph indicates that deep breathing techniques are the most commonly used method by students to reduce stress, followed by meditation and exercise.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students and displays a simple image of someone meditating or exercising. The teacher asks, "What are these people doing? How do they feel?" and "What words did we learn last class about feeling good?" (e.g., *energized, calm, healthy*). Students share their ideas in pairs. The teacher then writes the word "BENEFITS" on the board and asks, "What are the *benefits* of these activities?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher shows a very simple bar chart (e.g., "Favorite Fruits in Class X") and models how to quickly identify the main idea and key information (e.g., "The main idea is favorite fruits. Bananas are the most popular."). The teacher also models the pronunciation of key vocabulary from the previous lesson, focusing on stress in multisyllabic words like "mindfulness" and "flexibility."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is a 'benefit'? Is it something good or bad?" (Expected answer: "Good.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'reading a bar chart,' am I talking or looking?" (Expected answer: "Looking/reading.")</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Students are provided with a handout containing three items:
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">A short informational text (approx. 100 words) titled "Mindfulness and Your Well-being," introducing the *benefits* of *mindfulness* practice for *mental* and *physical* *health*. It includes sentences like "Mindfulness has many *benefits* for your *mental well-being*."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">A simple bar chart titled "Top Stress-Reduction Techniques for Teenagers," displaying data on *meditation*, *exercise*, *deep breathing*, and *relaxation* as methods. (e.g., "The graph indicates that deep breathing techniques are the most commonly used method by students to reduce stress.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">A basic "Daily Wellness Check-in" form, asking for *name*, *age*, *hours of sleep*, and a checkbox for feeling *energized* or *calm*. (e.g., "On the health check-in form, I need to fill out my name, age, and how many hours I sleep each night.").</li>
                </ul>
                The text and labels on the chart/form integrate vocabulary (e.g., *wellness, energy, improve, focus, routine*) and grammar (Present simple). The teacher ensures students can distinguish long and short vowels in words like *suit* and *soot* when reading.
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading comprehension – Understanding general meaning and specific information from short texts, diagrams, and forms.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read the text "Mindfulness and Your Well-being." Gist Question: "What is the article mainly about?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students look at the bar chart. True/False: "More teenagers use exercise than meditation to reduce stress."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students examine the "Daily Wellness Check-in" form. Matching: "Match the fields on the form (Name, Hours of Sleep) to what information you need to provide."</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Reading)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading specific details from graphs and forms. Practicing Present simple for general statements and collocations with health-related verbs (e.g., *energy boost*). Vocabulary: *wellness, benefits, improve, strengthen, mental well-being, relaxation, focus, habit*.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Reading for Gaps):</b> Students receive sentences with blanks related to the article and bar chart. Example: "Mindfulness can help to ______ your *focus* and *promote mental well-being*." (boost/improve). "According to the chart, ______ is the most popular *stress-reduction technique*." (deep breathing). Students fill in the blanks using words from a provided word bank that includes target vocabulary.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-Controlled Practice (Reading for Outline):</b> The teacher provides a simplified bar chart (e.g., "Benefits of Exercise for Students" showing *strength*, *flexibility*, *energy boost*, *improved focus*). In pairs, students read the chart and collaboratively complete sentences to outline its information. Example: "The chart shows that regular *exercise* can ______ our *strength* and *flexibility*. It also gives an *energy boost*." They also practice the difference between /ʊ/ in "could" and /uː/ in "cooed" by reading sentences provided by the teacher, such as "He *could* find a *suit* that fits."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Reading & Reporting):</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives two different mock "wellness forms" (e.g., "Morning Routine Check" and "Evening Habits Form"). They read both forms and identify three common types of information requested (e.g., *personal information*, *daily routines*, *feelings*). One student reads aloud a specific field from one form (e.g., "How many hours do you sleep?"), and the other student identifies the category of information. They practice sharing wellness practices like: "I *exercise* three times a week to stay fit and *focused*." and "Some people like to *meditate* because it *helps* them *manage stress*."</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task in a realistic scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Group "Wellness Research Project" - Students will work in small groups (3-4) to analyze new data and complete a wellness profile.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Description:</b> Each group receives two new documents:
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">A bar chart titled "Student Participation in Wellness Activities" (e.g., *physical education*, *extracurricular activities* like *baseball*, *soccer*, *volleyball*, and *folkloric dance*).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">A blank "Student Wellness Profile" form requiring entries for *name*, *favorite wellness activity*, *how often they practice*, and *two benefits they experience*.</li>
                </ul>
                First, groups read and discuss the bar chart, outlining the main information and identifying key points about student participation and benefits. (e.g., "The graph indicates that *physical education* and *soccer* are popular. These *activities* help *boost energy* and *improve strength*."). They use present simple for general statements.
                Next, they collaboratively fill out the "Student Wellness Profile" form for a hypothetical student, drawing information and vocabulary from the chart and their general knowledge of *wellness*. They consider what *routine* this student *maintains* and what *benefits* they are *experiencing*.
                Finally, each group presents a brief summary of their findings to another group, outlining the main information from the chart and the completed wellness profile. They engage in group discussions on wellness routines, contributing suggestions and solutions to promote *mental* and *physical well-being*. They can use sentences like "Our student is *practicing mindfulness* every day and *feeling calm*." (Present continuous) and "This *habit* helps *enhance their focus*."
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during group work and brief review of submitted forms/outlines.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their group's completed "Student Wellness Profile" form and a written outline of 3-4 key points from the "Student Participation in Wellness Activities" bar chart. Each group orally presents 1-2 key findings from their analysis.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity in outlining information from the bar chart (e.g., identifying top activities, understanding *benefits*).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct recognition and appropriate use of language on the "Student Wellness Profile" form.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (e.g., *wellness, mindfulness, exercise, benefits, strength, mental well-being, improve, focus*).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation when presenting (e.g., correct stress on "flexibility," "resilience").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaboration within the group.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a short exit ticket with the following prompts:
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can now understand (circle one): a little / some / most of the information in a bar chart."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I found it easy to recognize the language on a form asking for personal information about wellness. Yes / No"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-8 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most interesting *benefit* of *mindfulness* or *exercise* you learned about today from the readings?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can reading and understanding diagrams about *wellness* help you make *healthy* choices?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The reading activities effectively engaged students in outlining information from charts and recognizing form language. The vocabulary was well-integrated, and students showed understanding of key terms like *benefits* and *mental well-being*. Some students found it challenging to articulate precise outlines from the charts, so for the next lesson (Speaking), I will provide more sentence starters and specific prompts related to discussing data points. I also noted that practicing stress in words like "*resilience*" will be beneficial to continue. The minimal pair drills (/ʊ/ vs /uː/) were a good warm-up for reading accuracy.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> In the next lesson (Lesson 3: Speaking & Interaction), students will use the information and vocabulary learned today to actively discuss their own *wellness routines* and *benefits* they experience, engaging in peer interviews and collaborative discussions, further *strengthening* their communicative skills related to *wellness*.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>